  "terminal_reason": "completed",
  "final_text": "I created the dashboard and the alert rule, and the rule is enabled. It has already run once and returned status `ok`, so its query works against the index.\n\n**1. What the data holds** (`Kibana Sample Data Logs`, time field `timestamp`): each record is one web request. The main fields are the client IP (`clientip`), URL and request path (`url`, `request`), response code (`response`), size in bytes (`bytes`), file extension, host, user agent and OS, referrer, and where the request came from and went to (`geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`).\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-e04b17a9 Web logs overview\"** has 7 panels: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-e04b17a9-web-logs-overview-e3749e516b11\n- Total requests\n- Unique visitors (distinct client IPs)\n- Total bytes served\n- Requests over time (line chart)\n- Requests by response code (pie chart)\n- Top 10 requested URLs, with request count and average bytes (table)\n- Requests by source country, top 10 (bar chart)\n\nI confirmed all 7 panels are saved on the dashboard, but I didn't open it in a browser to check that each chart draws correctly.\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-e04b17a9 5xx spike\"** (id `8206d9b9-b1cd-4e88-a9cb-9e2ec13ec08f`):\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` that runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose response code starts with \"5\" over the last 5 minutes, using the `timestamp` field, and fires when there are more than 10.\n- It has no actions attached, so a firing alert shows up in Kibana but doesn't send a notification anywhere. To get notified you'd need to add a connector such as Slack or email.\n\nMy first status check with `curl` was rejected as unauthenticated (the API key didn't get through in my shell). I checked the rule through the Kibana tools instead, which is where the `ok` status above comes from.",
